WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.800
6,000 U.S. job ads still require a political essay a judge has barred. This is Omni View North America, episode 7. I'm Mira, the Nerra Network's AI host. On September 11th, Judge George A. O'Toole Jr. stayed that prompt. Applicants had to say how they would push the president's agenda and name executive orders they liked. He called the requirement likely unconstitutional. A Friday filing from the Justice Department says personal offices cannot

00:00:29.800 --> 00:00:35.900
put the essay on fresh announcements. Someone in the lawsuit told HuffPost it still turned up on five

00:00:35.900 --> 00:00:41.980
listings after the stay, Dawgons reports. Officials say wiping the old language by hand would break

00:00:41.980 --> 00:00:48.000
the hiring system, so agencies must ignore whatever answers come in. The replacement question asks

00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:53.460
about a time someone followed a boss against their own advice. The White House first required the essay

00:00:53.460 --> 00:00:58.200
in May 2025, then made it optional after civil servants pushed back.

00:00:58.740 --> 00:01:01.720
Ethics groups he'd said public hiring has to rest on merit.

00:01:02.320 --> 00:01:06.160
Across the region, Ontario's premier told a Calgary lunch on Monday,

00:01:06.560 --> 00:01:10.080
he will help pay for a pipeline that never has to cross American soil.

00:01:10.700 --> 00:01:14.480
Doug Ford said the province can put up patient capital for northern shield,

00:01:14.960 --> 00:01:19.180
built to move as much as 500,000 barrels a day from Hardesty to Sarnia.

00:01:19.660 --> 00:01:22.620
He told reporters Ontario will not cover the whole bill,

00:01:22.620 --> 00:01:26.220
and wants outside investors in, the Financial Post reports.

00:01:26.920 --> 00:01:30.880
A study of cost and how to commercialize the oil is due before this year ends.

00:01:31.420 --> 00:01:34.580
Back in July, standing with Alberta Premier Daniel Smith,

00:01:35.000 --> 00:01:38.820
he said Ontario could own the line and pay to build it if that was required.

00:01:39.420 --> 00:01:45.820
Smith said only 3% of Alberta's $188 billion in sales to the United States face the tariffs,

00:01:46.300 --> 00:01:49.240
against about 20% of Ontario's $500 billion.

00:01:49.240 --> 00:01:56.100
Washington put 50% duties on a range of Canadian goods in August, and Ottawa matched them on $28

00:01:56.100 --> 00:02:02.260
billion of American imports. In Nashville, state Senator Charlene Oliver has sued Lieutenant

00:02:02.260 --> 00:02:07.440
Governor Randy McNally over the punishment that followed her floor protest. He took her

00:02:07.440 --> 00:02:12.700
off-government operations, shifted her to state and local government, and cut her daily allowance

00:02:12.700 --> 00:02:18.340
in state-paid conference travel. The Tennessee lookout reports. In a May special session, she stood

00:02:18.340 --> 00:02:23.020
on her Senate desk and opened a banner while lawmakers redrew the U.S. House map.

00:02:23.540 --> 00:02:28.120
The lookout says Republicans split Memphis into three districts and are favored in all

00:02:28.120 --> 00:02:28.760
nine seats.

00:02:29.460 --> 00:02:33.560
All of her said in a release she acted because she believed lawmakers were silencing

00:02:33.560 --> 00:02:34.620
black Tennesseans.

00:02:35.240 --> 00:02:38.320
McMalley's spokesperson had not answered by publication time.

00:02:38.800 --> 00:02:43.480
The new lines followed an April U.S. Supreme Court ruling that states with a record of racial

00:02:43.480 --> 00:02:47.260
discrimination no longer had to draw a majority minority House seats.

00:02:47.260 --> 00:02:53.620
Also today, MinPose says Republicans left 17 Minnesota House races and four Senate races

00:02:53.620 --> 00:02:56.120
empty, with the DFL on every ballot.

00:02:56.780 --> 00:03:01.180
NPR says the Trump administration rolled back fuel efficiency standards for new vehicles.

00:03:01.820 --> 00:03:06.360
The New York Times reports Lindsey Clancy's lawyers want a not guilty finding after this

00:03:06.360 --> 00:03:09.660
month's mistrial, plus an inquiry into a holdout juror.

00:03:10.000 --> 00:03:15.440
In the wider world, NPR says Turkey is ready to step in as the United States signals a smaller

00:03:15.440 --> 00:03:20.140
Middle East footprint. The piece sets that bid against shocks that opened with the Hamas-led

00:03:20.140 --> 00:03:26.300
attack on Israel in 2023. It says those shocks run on in a war on Iran that has been stuck for

00:03:26.300 --> 00:03:31.920
seven months. The case on both sides of whether Alberta should leave Canada is now a stack of rival

00:03:31.920 --> 00:03:37.720
papers ahead of an October 19th ballot with 10 questions. Tyler Dawson writes in the Globe and Mail

00:03:37.720 --> 00:03:43.100
that forever Canadian has switched to getting Federalists out to vote. The provincial government is

00:03:43.100 --> 00:03:44.200
running ads of its own.

00:03:44.640 --> 00:03:47.280
Premier Danielle Smith has argued against separation

00:03:47.280 --> 00:03:49.360
while also pressing what Dawson calls

00:03:49.360 --> 00:03:51.040
an emotional list of grievances.

00:03:51.580 --> 00:03:53.780
Eight authors at the Canada West Foundation

00:03:53.780 --> 00:03:56.820
say no one has settled Alberta's share of Canada's debt

00:03:56.820 --> 00:03:59.900
and that any share would drive the provincial debt up hard.

00:04:00.460 --> 00:04:03.020
They also ask how lines around First Nations lands

00:04:03.020 --> 00:04:03.640
would be drawn.

00:04:04.240 --> 00:04:05.960
The Alberta Transition Council says

00:04:05.960 --> 00:04:08.140
a secession vote would not collapse the world,

00:04:08.440 --> 00:04:10.940
that every side has reason to keep a split friendly

00:04:10.940 --> 00:04:13.540
and that daily life would not be thrown into chaos.

00:04:14.140 --> 00:04:16.920
It has issued a paper it calls a budget and a plan.

00:04:17.500 --> 00:04:19.880
Dawson thinks those documents will move few readers,

00:04:20.300 --> 00:04:23.060
because in his view, the votes are mostly locked in.

00:04:23.560 --> 00:04:25.660
A sign of progress at Canada's top court

00:04:25.660 --> 00:04:27.540
is a higher share of appeals accepted,

00:04:28.020 --> 00:04:28.980
the Globe and Mail reports.

00:04:29.760 --> 00:04:32.840
Judges have said yes to 13% of applications this year,

00:04:33.260 --> 00:04:35.360
against 7% through the 2020s.

00:04:35.860 --> 00:04:39.700
Two more grants last week put 2026 at 41 new cases.

00:04:39.700 --> 00:04:46.400
the most since 42 in 2018. Daniel Baima, chief of staff to Chief Justice Richard Wagner,

00:04:46.920 --> 00:04:51.220
said the court sets no yearly target. Justice Mahmoud Jamal said in February,

00:04:51.720 --> 00:04:56.380
they are not trying to hear fewer cases. The decade's average is still 34 a year,

00:04:56.760 --> 00:05:03.020
beside 52 in the 2010s. That's OmniView North America for today. I'm Mira. See you tomorrow.

